From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ARM64EC: opt-in switches for programs that rewrite their own code all
 the time

Forza Horizon 6's copy protection rebuilds a 16 MB ring of stubs and traps through it 13,000 to 20,000 times a second.
Every switch here is off unless its variable is set, so nothing changes for other programs:

HB_FEX_SMC_INLINE=1 with HB_FEX_SMC_INLINE_MS, _MAX, _KEEP_READ, _RETIRE_PROT, _RETIRE_UNMAP, _RETIRE_FREE and
_BLOCK_REMOVE: a page written twice within the window stops being write-protected and each block on it checks its own
bytes where it starts, so a write costs nothing and only code that really changed is translated again.
HB_FEX_PRIVATE_CHURN=N: a thread past N translations of writable code moves to code buffers of its own, so its churn no
longer fills the shared buffer and makes every thread translate again.
HB_FEX_FAST_LEGACY_UD=1, HB_FEX_FAST_PREFIX_UD=1 and HB_FEX_DIRECT_SIGILL=1: an opcode that always faults in long mode
raises the guest exception without a translation or a host trap.
HB_FEX_CALLRET_EPOCH=1: the call-return stack is invalidated by an epoch instead of madvise.
HB_FEX_SMC_RETIRE_FAULTS=1 and HB_FEX_SMC_CACHE_FILTER=1: blocks with failed decodes can be retired and reinstated, and
invalidation skips cache walks for pages that hold no translated code.
HB_FEX_MAPJIT=1: the thread's write toggle for Wine's MAP_JIT buffers (Wine patch 0052).

Many of the counters kept under FEX_PROFILESTATS reuse existing slots of the shared statistics and are for diagnosis only.
Measured on a base M4, macOS 27.0: Forza Horizon 6 in free roam, no frame over 80 ms from the protection (2026-10-31).
Not for upstream: FEX does not take LLM-written code.
